          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of an
            %em elementary (∞,1)-topos
            is an analogue of the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            %em Grothendieck
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalent
                to an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-category of (∞,1)-sheaves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+homotopy+type+theory"} geometric homotopy type theory
          %p#AsCategoricalSemanticsForHoTT
            Note that every
            %em Grothendieck
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
            provides
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types+of+types"} types of types
            and also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
            (HITs) – see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory#HomotopyWithUnivalence"} here
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %em all
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for such homotopy type theory. More precisely, we will define an elementary
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence+theorem"} coherence theorem
            making it
            %em actually
            model the corresponding type theory is still unknown (but see at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initiality+conjecture"} initiality conjecture
          %p
            In general, the problem with “elementary-izing” the notion of
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebras+for+endofunctors"} initial algebras for endofunctors
            using a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+induction"} transfinite induction
            argument; thus in type theory we postulate the existence of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+types"} inductive types
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+type"} higher inductive type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HITs"} HITs
            we ask for.
          %h2#definition_impredicative_case Definition (impredicative case)
          %p
            There are at least two ambiguities in the above sketch of a definition based on type theory: what exactly do we assume of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"}> object classifiers
            ), and what general class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HITs"} HITs
            do we ask for? One reasonable answer to the universe question is that every object should be contained in a universe closed under all the relevant operations, and one reasonable answer to the HITs question is just the non-recursive ones. This leads to the following definition.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              An
              %strong
                elementary
                = succeed "-topos" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              is an
              = succeed "-category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
                  has finite
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-limit"} limits and colimits
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed
              %li
                %p
                  There exists a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
                  (an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
                  that classifies the collection of all
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} monomorphisms in an (∞,1)-category
              %li
                %p
                  For any morphism
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi Y
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:Y\to X
                  in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi E
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
                  classifying a class of morphisms that (1) includes
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  and (2) is closed under fiberwise finite limits and colimits, composition (i.e. dependent sums), and dependent products.
          %p
            Note that this definition is “impredicative” just like an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            %em all
            subobjects. We can’t categorify this to a classifier for all objects due to size paradoxes; the existence of “enough” object classifiers closed under all the basic operations is the “next best thing”. We will refer to an object classifier satisfying (2) above as a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong universe
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            It is reasonable to hope for a coherence theorem showing that any elementary
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+type"} HITs
            (corresponding to the finite colimits), univalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"} universes
            (perhaps only weakly Tarski ones), and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+resizing"} propositional resizing
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/homotopytypetheory/show/model+of+type+theory+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} model of type theory in an (infinity,1)-topos
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory#HomotopyWithUnivalence"} relation between type theory and category theory – Univalent homotopy type theory and infinity-toposes
          %p
            In an elementary 1-topos we can construct finite colimits and dependent exponentials from non-dependent exponentials and the subobject classifier (or equivalently from
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+objects"} power objects
            = succeed "-case" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The initial object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+initial+object"} strict
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to 0
              is an equivalence.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Just as for 1-categories, this is true in any cartesian closed
              = succeed "-category." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to 0
              then the projection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times 0 \to A
              has a section
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1_A,f)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is a retract of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times 0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≅
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times 0 \cong 0
              by cartesian closedness since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A\times -)
              has a right adjoint and hence preserves colimits.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Binary coproducts in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
              are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+coproducts"} disjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i:A\to A+B
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j:B\to A+B
              are monic, and their pullback is initial.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This is also just like a corresponding proof for 1-toposes (see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes+are+extensive"} toposes are extensive
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              to make the following squares pullbacks:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi R
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi r
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mi s
                          %mtd
                            %mi S
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mrow
                                %mi r
                                %mo ′
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi i
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %mo +
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{ R &amp; \xrightarrow{r} &amp; A &amp; \xleftarrow{s} &amp; S\\
                    ^{r'}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^i &amp;&amp; \downarrow\\
                    A &amp; \xrightarrow{i} &amp; A+B &amp; \xleftarrow{j} &amp; B}
            %p
              Specifically,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mi r
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r,r')
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kernel+pair"} kernel pair
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▵
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangle : A \to R
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ▵
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \triangle = 1_A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
              is locally cartesian closed, pullback preserves colimits, so
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r,s)
              is a coproduct diagram. Thus, the pair of morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi R
                    %mo :
                    %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_R:R\to R
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ▵
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi s
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \triangle \circ s : S\to R
              factor (uniquely) through some
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h:A\to R
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mi r
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h r = 1_R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mi R
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r:R\to A
              has both a left and a right inverse, so it is an equivalence, and hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is monic. Dually,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              is monic.
            %p
              Now we claim that any two morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:S\to X
              with domain
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              are equivalent. For the pair of morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi r
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \xrightarrow{r} A \to A+X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \xrightarrow{f} X \to A+X
              factor uniquely through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              (which, recall, is the coproduct
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo +
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R+S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is an equivalence, this factorization must be (equivalent to) the left coprojection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo +
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to A+X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi f
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \xrightarrow{f} X \to A+X
              is equivalent to the composite
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi s
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo +
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \xrightarrow{s} A \to A+X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                      %mo +
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \xrightarrow{g} X \to A+X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo +
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to A+X
              is monic by the previous paragraph,
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\simeq g
            %p
              Finally, since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is a strict initial object,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\to S
              is monic. And of course
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi S
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_S : S\to S
              is also monic, and these two monomorphisms are classified by two maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g:S\to \Omega
              into the subobject classifier. By the previous paragraph,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\simeq g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo ≅
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\cong 0
              and is initial.
          %p
            In fact, disjoint binary coproducts can actually be constructed from local cartesian closure and the subobject classifier; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FreyRasekh"} FreyRasekh
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            can be constructed from the rest of the structure, but it is unclear whether it can be generalized beyond coproducts in the infinite-dimensional case. In particular, it is shown in
            %a{:href => "#FreyRasekh"} FreyRasekh
            that local cartesian closure and the subobject classifier do
            %em not
            suffice to construct pushouts; the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              For any finite family of morphisms
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f_i : Y_i \to X_i\}_{i\in I}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i
              is the pullback of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_i f_i
              along the injection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_i \to \sum_i X_i
              (this follows from extensivity), a universe classifying
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_i f_i
              must also classify each
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_i
          %p In particular, we have “universe cumulativity”:
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              For any universe
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo :
                      %mi V
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:V\to U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p':V'\to U'
              that classifies both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\to 1
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              All finite colimits are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+colimit"} van Kampen
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
              is locally cartesian closed, all colimits are pullback-stable, so it suffices to show descent. Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is a finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊳
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mo ⊳
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mo ⊳
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\rhd:G^\rhd:D^\rhd \to \mathcal{E}
              are colimiting cones under diagrams
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℰ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G:D\to \mathcal{E}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mo ⊳
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊳
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mo ⊳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^\rhd : F\rhd \to G^\rhd
              be a natural transformation whose restriction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:F\to G : D\to \mathcal{E}
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equifibered+natural+transformation"} equifibered
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:V\to U
              be a universe classifying
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_d : F_d \to G_d
              for each vertex
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi D
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\in D_0
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is the pullback of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              along a cocone
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi q
                      %mo :
                      %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q:G\to U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mi α
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r : \alpha \to p
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^\to
              which is equifibered.
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mo ⊳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^\rhd
              is colimiting under
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi ℰ
                        %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:D\to \mathcal{E}^\to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mo ⋆
                      %mo →
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_\star \to p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mo ⊳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star\in D^\rhd
              is the cocone vertex. But since colimits are pullback-stable, the pullback of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi G
                      %mo ⊳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G^\rhd
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              is a colimiting cocone under
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi F
                        %mo ⊳
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\rhd
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi α
                      %mo ⊳
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha^\rhd
              is equifibered.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p
              There exists a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
          %p
            Intuitively, it seems as if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega(S^1)
            (which can be constructed using finite limits and colimits) ought to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            and we should be able to construct the natural numbers from it; but it is unclear to the authors of this page how to do that. Instead, we can (using the impredicative subobject classifier) define the smallest subobject of an object classifier
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:V\to U
            that contains the initial object and is closed under taking coproducts with the terminal object. This is almost right, but it is not 0-truncated. We could 0-truncate it, but to get a universal property relative to all objects rather than only 0-truncated ones, we can instead impose structure making the objects rigid, such as a partial order or a graph. This argument is formalized in type theory
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#ConstructingNat"} here
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_6 Theorem
            %p
              There is an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n-connected%2C+n-truncated%29+factorization+system"} (n-connected, n-truncated) factorization system
          %p
            The case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=-1
            can be constructed impredicatively using the subobject classifier: internally,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert A \Vert
            is the smallest
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
            implied by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em external
            natural numbers, defining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert A \Vert_{n+1}
            to be the
            = succeed "-image" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to U^A \to (U')^A
            = succeed "-truncation." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-truncation" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+2
            universe levels”. However, unlike type theory, an elementary
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
